"Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172097 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172097 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172098 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186173008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1045 "Path Independence of Line Integrals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172180 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "da0a118d-376a-5230-a1dc-46ce7816f56d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172299 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460621 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172299 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460622 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172299 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460623 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172299 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460624 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172299 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172299 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172299 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is conservative." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172299 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41a70c70-f13c-564f-a1cc-c8837528e651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172299 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172303 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172303 #edb/ref 1069 "q-125760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172303 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172303 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172303 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25a22695-171f-55bf-b6a5-1f3b602faf0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172303 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172927 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172927 #edb/ref 1061 32376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172927 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16937ae1-27e8-5337-99ff-6a5dd1b9e446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172927 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172932 #edb/ref 1061 32601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cab8518e-c3d7-56ce-a74a-1da8815349ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173007 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186173008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173007 #edb/ref 1061 35770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a55b03af-55fa-5584-8ba8-abdc7dda9f46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173008 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173008 #edb/ref 1061 35774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173008 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d161b477-2d2f-52f2-881b-e7e6f77d4ecc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173169 #edb/ref 1064 "The fundamental theorem of line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is a piecewise-smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and $f$ is differentiable function such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ \n\nThis theorem tells us that the line integral of a conservative vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla f$ is determined by the values of $f$ at the endpoints $A$ and $B$ of $C$ only. The integral does *not* depend on the path between $A$ and $B.$ \n\nIn particular, if $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, then we say that $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$ is **path-independent.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173169 #edb/ref 1065 5145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173169 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ab2cc7cb-1fa4-5e9c-92d4-551aeb945c53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173169 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256819 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921862